Z||000000||FullRaceCam||09-26-2001||06:50 PM||||  I wrote a  while ago about shifting problems. I dropped the cradle yesterday and today tore the transaxle off. Found that my clutch had started to fall apart. Two springs were loose/free. <BR>  Originally couldn't get car in gear when running and clutch seemed to hang up a little, stiff then free. <BR>  Now... the input shaft has wear on the splines on the leading edge(presumed leading edge). Each spline edge has a proud lip on it. A buddy says this could cause enough binding for the clutch to bang together even though I tend to (generally) be easy on the clutch when engaging. It is the source of the hangup I was experiencing. Car has 250k kilometers(160k miles) and that is probably normal wear on the shaft.<BR>  Two things I'm looking for now. 1) Is there a site with an exploded diagram of the 88 transaxle. I'm going to have to replace the input shaft and I'm not looking forward to it. Even if I can get the part up here in Canada. 2) I also just read at the FieroFactory(?) that the flywheel should not be surfaced when replacing a clutch due to increased distance of clutch travel. I had this done when I replaced the clutch two years ago and until now doesn't seem to have caused problems and I'm not sure if this is related. Is the alternative to purchase a new flywheel each time?<BR>  Oh, by the way, I mentioned in the previous post that I couldn't remember if the input shaft bearng had a sleeve. Yes it does, no visible wear, 0.006 clearance to throwout brg. <BR>  Any responses will be great, I'll check back every once in a while. I'm going surfing for pictures. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||87GTZ34||09-26-2001||10:10 PM||||Rodney Dickman will sell you what you need cheaply enough.  I bought a manual although I haven't used it yet . GL||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||09-26-2001||10:16 PM||||I buy a new flywheel each time I replace a clutch. In my Formula's case that is 2 flywheels so far, at $250/piece. They end up in the trash once I pull 'em. I'm not going to waste my time with resurfacing and hoping hoping to have no problems afterward. I'd buy a new one, especially since it has already been under the knife once.||||reg||1
